#%PAM-1.0
#
# IRN OS override of the stock greetd PAM stack.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: greetd here only ever AUTOLOGINS the fixed, unprivileged user
# `irn` (see /etc/greetd/config.toml -> [initial_session]). There is no
# interactive greeter and no password is ever typed, so greetd's job is to start
# one known session, not to guard a login.
#
# The stock file does `@include login`, whose auth stack is `pam_unix.so nullok`.
# An [initial_session] has no greeter to answer PAM's password conversation, so
# pam_unix gets nothing to check and the session never starts. Permit auth for
# this autologin-only service, but keep the normal account/session handling so
# pam_systemd still registers the seat and exports XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (the session
# script relies on it — see /usr/local/bin/irn-kiosk).
#
# NOT the same bug as the "could not authenticate" black console: that one was
# the `irn` user not existing at all, because the `user-setup` package was
# missing and live-config's user-setup component silently no-ops without it.
# See config/package-lists/live.list.chroot. This file alone does not fix that —
# greetd cannot log in a user that does not exist.
#
# SECURITY NOTE: `auth required pam_permit.so` means greetd performs no password
# check. That is acceptable ONLY because this service exclusively autologins the
# unprivileged desktop user on a machine whose real boundary is LUKS + the
# Sanctuary vaults, not the session login. Do NOT copy this stack to any greetd
# instance that offers an interactive login.

auth       required   pam_permit.so
account    required   pam_permit.so

session    required   pam_limits.so
session    optional   pam_env.so readenv=1
session    optional   pam_env.so readenv=1 envfile=/etc/default/locale
@include common-session
